  "authoritative_metadata": "manifest.json",
  "title": "Hidden points and the two-bit screening lemma",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "The two-bit screening lemma: its no-loss half proved, the rest reduced to one statement. (1) The elimination closure behind creation is a tetrahedron rule: the least set of class triples containing the non-crossing knots in which every four classes with two triples in the set have all their knot triples in it. (2) No-loss theorem: let an intact side split from the Fano plane at a point be attached at a line L0. At every removal and gluing line off the side, and for every far side, the new knots of up to three points that avoid the side are exactly those of the space with L0 restored, a generated knot through a hidden point has generated port shadows, and no crossing knot meets two hidden classes; so the side never removes creation. The same holds for one new point on one new line through two near points (a pendant line). (3) That the side never adds creation follows from the pendant-line statement that a pendant line never adds creation, which holds on 117,344 cases (and the side's lemma itself on 28,288) but is neither local nor a consequence of the knots alone; it is stated as a conjecture. (4) The bound is sharp: a point on two or three new lines through a line's points (8 of the 27 one-point profiles, majorities and parity copies of the ports) can add creation, and no knot-space side tried (the census, prisms, every three-fold space up to 22 points) has such a point.",
